    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>A concrete driveway is more than somewhere to park the car. It is a substantial investment that shapes the kerb appeal of your home and has to carry heavy loads in every kind of weather. Installed well, it can give many years of reliable service. When corners are cut or technical mistakes are made during planning and pouring, though, the results can be expensive.</p>
<p>Plenty of homeowners end up with a driveway that starts to crack, flake or sink within a few years of being laid. The material itself is rarely to blame; the cause is usually an avoidable mistake during installation. Understanding the basics of good concrete work helps you ask the right questions and spot shortcuts. Below are the most costly mistakes and how to make sure your driveway stands the test of time.</p>

<h2 id="subgrade">1. A poorly prepared sub-base</h2>
<p>The most common and most expensive mistakes happen before any concrete arrives. The subgrade is the ground the driveway sits on, and some low-budget installers pour straight onto loose soil or ground that has not been properly compacted.</p>
<p>Concrete is rigid and does not flex. If the ground underneath shifts, settles or washes away, the slab spans the gap until the weight of a vehicle snaps it, leaving deep structural cracks that are hard to repair. To avoid this, the site should be dug out to remove turf, roots and other organic material, which rot and leave voids. A layer of compacted crushed stone (sub-base) then goes down, compacted in layers with a vibrating plate or roller; depths of around 100–150 mm (4–6 in) are common, but your contractor should set the depth for your soil and how the driveway will be used. A firm, even base gives uniform support and prevents the settling that leads to replacement later.</p>

<h2 id="mix-ratio">2. A watery mix</h2>
<p>Not all concrete is the same. A frequent mistake is a "soupy" mix with too much water added on site. Water is needed for the chemical reaction that hardens concrete, but too much of it weakens the result. A wetter mix is easier to spread and level, which is why it can be tempting on the day, but as the extra water leaves the slab it leaves tiny voids behind.</p>
<p>That more porous concrete is weaker and much more vulnerable to frost. Water gets into the pores, freezes and expands, and the surface starts to flake away. Ask your contractor which mix they are ordering, and make sure it is specified for an external driveway in your climate rather than a general-purpose mix. If someone suggests adding extra water to make the concrete easier to work, question it: it trades the long-term durability of your driveway for convenience on the day.</p>

<h2 id="joint-placement">3. Badly placed joints</h2>
<p>Concrete always shrinks slightly as it hardens, and that shrinkage builds tension in the slab. Left unmanaged, it relieves itself as random, web-like cracks. Control joints are planned weak lines, cut or tooled into the surface, that tell the concrete where to crack so that any cracking stays neat and hidden in the joint.</p>
<p>Joints that are too far apart or too shallow are a common fault. Installers commonly work to guidelines linking joint spacing and depth to the slab's thickness, and the contractor should set the joint layout for your particular slab and show it on the plan. Leaving out isolation (expansion) joints where the driveway meets the house, garage floor or a path is another costly error: without that flexible buffer, the slab can push against walls and steps as it expands in hot weather and damage both.</p>

<h2 id="reinforcement">4. Skipping or misplacing reinforcement</h2>
<p>Concrete is very strong when it is squashed, but much weaker when it is pulled or bent. Reinforcement helps it cope. Some driveways are poured with none to save on materials, and others have steel mesh that ends up lying at the bottom of the slab, where it does little good.</p>
<p>Where reinforcement is used, whether steel mesh or bars, it must be raised on spacers (often called chairs) so it sits within the thickness of the slab once the concrete is poured, not trodden into the base. Fibre-reinforced concrete, with fine synthetic fibres mixed through it, can also help limit small surface cracks. Whether your driveway needs reinforcement, and what kind, depends on the ground and the vehicles it will carry, so ask your contractor or an engineer to specify it rather than leaving it out by default.</p>

<h2 id="curing">5. Rushing the curing</h2>
<p>Curing is not the same as drying. Drying is water evaporating; curing is the chemical process that gives concrete its strength, and it needs moisture. It is one of the most neglected stages, because a new driveway looks hard and dry within a day or two and it is tempting to start using it.</p>
<p>If the surface dries out too fast in sun or wind, it ends up weak and dusty and wears quickly. The usual remedies are to keep the slab damp and covered with sheeting for the first days, or to spray on a curing compound that holds the moisture in; follow your contractor's advice on which and for how long. Keep off it as long as they advise too: cars usually need to wait at least a week, and heavier vehicles considerably longer. Impatience at this stage is a leading cause of early surface failure.</p>

<h2 id="drainage">6. Ignoring drainage</h2>
<p>Water is concrete's quiet enemy. A driveway laid dead flat, or worse, sloping towards the house, is asking for trouble. Standing water soaks into the ground below, softening the base and leading to cracks; in winter puddles become ice patches, which are a slip hazard and speed up surface scaling.</p>
<p>A well-built driveway has a gentle fall away from the house so rainwater runs off; your installer will set the fall to suit the site. On sloping or awkward plots, channel drains along the edge or across the entrance may be needed. Planning matters here too: in England, a new or replacement hard surface over 5 m² in a front garden generally needs planning permission unless it is permeable or drains to a permeable area, such as a lawn or soakaway, within the property, and a new dropped kerb needs council permission. In the US, check local permits and HOA rules. Check with your local authority before work starts, as getting drainage wrong can mean water pooling against walls or a retrospective planning problem.</p>

<h2 id="contractor-selection">7. Choosing the cheapest contractor</h2>
<p>The appeal of the lowest quote may be the most expensive mistake of all. Concrete work is labour-intensive and needs proper equipment. If one quote is far lower than the others, ask what is different: it may be a thinner slab, a cheaper mix, less reinforcement or a shallower base.</p>
<p>Before you hire anyone:</p>
<ul>
<li>Check that they hold suitable public liability insurance, and ask to see the certificate.</li>
<li>Ask for references from recent customers and the addresses of driveways they laid some years ago, so you can see how their work has aged.</li>
<li>Insist on a written, itemised quote that states the slab thickness, the sub-base depth and material, the type of reinforcement, the joint layout, drainage and who removes the waste.</li>
<li>In the UK, look for membership of a recognised trade association or government-endorsed trader scheme, and check the membership is current.</li>
<li>Ask what guarantee they offer and what it covers.</li>
</ul>
<p>Comparing quotes line by line on the same specification makes it far easier to see where the savings in a cheap bid really come from.</p>

<h2 id="sealing">8. Never sealing the surface</h2>
<p>Once a driveway is poured and cured, many people think the job is finished. Concrete is porous, though, and soaks up oil, salt and other chemicals. Where roads are salted in winter, salt carried in on tyres can work its way into the slab and, over time, corrode any steel reinforcement inside.</p>
<p>A good penetrating sealer, or a surface acrylic sealer if you prefer a slight sheen, adds a protective barrier. Ask your contractor how long the new concrete needs before it can be sealed (often around a month) and which product suits the finish. After that, reseal every few years or as the sealer label advises; once water stops beading on the surface, it is due. Sealing helps resist stains and reduces water getting in, and skipping it leaves the concrete exposed to the damage described above.</p>
<blockquote><p><strong>Tip:</strong> Ask your contractor to give you a short handover note with the mix, slab thickness, reinforcement, joint layout and the sealer they recommend. It makes future repairs, resealing and any guarantee claim much simpler.</p></blockquote>

<h2 id="faq">FAQ</h2>
<h3>How thick should a residential concrete driveway be?</h3>
<p>For ordinary cars, slabs are commonly around 100 mm (4 in) thick, and thicker where heavier vehicles such as vans, motorhomes or trailers will park. There is no single right answer: your contractor or a structural engineer should specify the thickness for your soil and use.</p>
<h3>Can I pour a new driveway over the old one to save money?</h3>
<p>It is possible (this is called an overlay), but it is often not a good idea. If the original slab cracked because of a problem in the ground underneath, the same cracks tend to show through the new layer quite quickly. Ask a contractor to assess the old slab; breaking it out and starting again is often the better long-term option.</p>
<h3>Why is my new concrete driveway turning white or dusty?</h3>
<p>A white bloom is usually efflorescence, natural salts carried to the surface by moisture, which often fades with time and can be cleaned with a suitable product. A powdery, dusty surface usually means it was finished while water was still rising to the surface or was not cured properly; ask the installer to look at it.</p>
<h3>When is the best time of year to pour a concrete driveway?</h3>
<p>Mild, settled weather is best. Very hot conditions make concrete set too quickly, which can lead to cracking, and freezing conditions can stop it curing properly. Your contractor will advise on suitable conditions and on protecting the slab if the weather turns.</p>
<h3>How soon can I drive on a new concrete driveway?</h3>
<p>Concrete can feel hard within a day, but it carries on gaining strength for weeks. Follow your contractor's advice: cars usually wait at least a week, and heavy vehicles such as delivery lorries or skips should stay off for considerably longer.</p>

<h2 id="final-thoughts">Final thoughts</h2>
<p>Most failed concrete driveways can be traced back to a few decisions made before and during the pour: the base, the mix, the joints, the reinforcement, the curing, the drainage and who does the work. Get those right and the driveway will look good for a long time. Once it is laid, our <a href="/blog/concrete-driveway-maintenance-by-season">seasonal concrete driveway maintenance</a> guide will help you keep it that way. If you are still deciding on a surface, compare the <a href="/blog/paver-driveway-pros-and-cons">pros and cons of a paver driveway</a> or read about common <a href="/blog/gravel-driveway-mistakes">gravel driveway mistakes</a>. For help designing the front garden around your new driveway, explore our <a href="/services/balcony-terrace-decoration">balcony and terrace decoration</a> service or <a href="/book-consultation">book a consultation</a>.</p>
